==English==

===Etymology===
{{suffix|en|impede|ance}}. In {{senseno|en|physics1}} and {{senseno|en|physics2}} it was {{coinage|en|Oliver Heaviside|in=1886|nat=English|occ=mathematician and physicist|nocap=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ɪmˈpiːdn̩s/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-impedance.wav|Audio (US)}}

{{wikipedia}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act of [[impede|impeding]]; that which impedes; a [[hindrance]].
#* '''2012''', R. Crafton Gibbs, ''Spirit Growth: Logos 2, Vol. 2, of Writing God's Book of Life'' (page 31)
#*: Faithful yielding is therefore a display of love before God by preparing to respond to his Call to Arms, even though He may not send us into the front lines of battle if some disability or '''impedance''' of Satan prevents us from action {{...}}
# {{senseid|en|physics1}} {{lb|en|physics}} A [[measure]] of the [[opposition]] to the [[flow]] of an [[alternating current]] in a [[circuit]]; the [[aggregation]] of its [[resistance]], and [[inductive]] and [[capacitive]] [[reactance]]s; the ratio of voltage to current treated as [[complex]] quantities.
# {{senseid|en|physics2}} {{lb|en|physics}} A quantity analogous to electrical impedance in some other energy domain
## {{lb|en|physics|usually with “mechanical”}} a measure of opposition to [[motion]] of something subjected to a force; the ratio of force to velocity treated as complex quantities.
## {{lb|en|physics|usually with “acoustic” or “sound”}} the ratio of sound pressure to volume flow rate treated as complex quantities.

====Usage notes====
Impedance is universally given the symbol ''Z'' in technical works which is often used as a synonym for the word even in running text.

